e to stop exploiting Africa this  word exploitation is very strange it  means taking advantage of a situation  and it's premise the negative  implication is premised on an assumption  that if I exploit you or you exploit me  and we both reduce we both participate  in that exploitation voluntarily that  one of us is somehow worse off because  the other one is better or for that's  clearly nonsense is economic inequality  going to be a problem for Africa the  world has just experienced in his  experience  the most extraordinary phenomenon that  has ever happened in the world the most  amazing accomplishment of humanity is  the virtual elimination of poverty and  it's strange that as that happens we are  talking about it as if there's more of  it and this is simply not so so rising  income inequality is using old-fashioned  econometric ways of measuring something  namely how much money you have or the  value of capital that you have or assets  but economists have never claimed that  this is telling you much about the  quality of life the poor use to live 20  21 25 years and on our they're in their  living standards are rising such access  to medical care food proper housing safe  water and so on to the point where they  are now living 65 70 years and age life  expectancies conflating for the rich and  the poor that's probably the single most  important indicator that in fact is  declining inequality the amenities of  life for the poor the access to  motorised transport to  telecommunications to medical care to  painkillers to food to education one  aspect of Africa's economy that's gotten  a fair amount of press in the 21st  century is the rise of the  telecommunications industry there and  you see pictures of Africans and what  appeared to be very poor villages  holding cell phone have you observed the  mobile technology revolution in Africa  and and what effect has it had there  I've traveled around for example in some  of the remotest parts of planet Earth a  Maasai Mara and south western Kenya for  example in northeastern Tanzania  Northwest in Tanzania and there you see  the iconic Messiah herdsmen in a red  blanket with a Nessa guy in one hand  herding some goats and a cellphone in  the other hand and they no longer carry  cash trader on the side of the road  under a tree will transfer the money  from when mobile phone to another in a  way that even Americans are not yet  doing you've actually said that digital  currencies like Bitcoin might be one of  the most important developments going  forward why do you feel that way so  being able to buy and sell and transfer  freely from one part of the world to  another without being watched or known  or documented is one of the great  Liberation's that the world might  experience without knowing the term all  the technology friedrich hayek i  consider myself more Hayekian than iock  predicted something like this in his  book the d nationalization of money  which was that they should be completely  non government money and had he known  about the technology for cryptocurrency  I'm quite sure that would have excited  him a great deal as a Nobel  prize-winning economist  Leon Lowe thank you very much for  talking to us my pleasure  for reason TV I'm Zack Weissmuller
 

